The fix is to add the tenant to each `WHERE` clause from `req.user.accountId`, which `auth.js` already sets. Then return 404 when no row matches.

`routes/orders.js`:

```js
router.patch("/:id/cancel", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled' WHERE id = $1 AND account_id = $2 RETURNING *",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});
```

`routes/accounts.js` works the same way, except the scope column is `id` itself, so a user can only change their own account:

```js
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 AND id = $4 RETURNING *",
    [req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});

router.delete("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET deleted_at = now() WHERE id = $1 AND id = $2 RETURNING id",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.status(204).end();
});
```

The 404 check is part of the fix. `db.write` returns `res.rows[0]`, so without the check, an id belonging to another account would still get a 200 with an empty body or a 204. Using 404 instead of 403 also avoids confirming that another account's id exists.

The main choice is whether to make scoping structural so future routes can't forget it. `db.write` is already the only place a transaction begins. It could take the account id and run `SELECT set_config('app.account_id', $1, true)` after `BEGIN`, and Postgres row-level security policies on `accounts` and `orders` would then enforce the scoping. This is worth doing if you expect many more routes. Two caveats: RLS doesn't apply to the table owner unless you set `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`, and reads through `query()` would need the same setting.

There's also an unrelated bug: `auth.js` reads `req.cookies`, but `app.js` never mounts `cookie-parser`. As written, every request gets a 401, so you won't be able to test the fix until that's added.

I'd add one middleware in `app.js` and mount it before `sessionUser`. It writes a log line when each response closes:

```js
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const start = process.hrtime.bigint();
  res.on("close", () => {
    console.log(JSON.stringify({
      method: req.method,
      path: req.originalUrl,
      status: res.statusCode,
      ms: Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - start) / 1e6,
      userId: req.user && req.user.id,
      accountId: req.user && req.user.accountId,
      aborted: !res.writableFinished,
    }));
  });
  next();
});
```

- **Before `sessionUser`:** rejected requests still get their 401 logged. `req.user` is only read when the response closes, so authenticated requests still include the user and account ids.
- **`close` instead of `finish`:** `close` also fires when the client gives up, so hung writes like the ones from the previous answer still show up.
- **No bodies or cookies:** request bodies include `billing_email`, and the cookie is the session token.

The open question is where the logs should go. JSON on stdout works as a default. Do you already use a logging stack? If it's pino, for example, `pino-http` would replace this middleware.

