```
{"reqId":"abc-123","method":"PATCH","path":"/orders/5/cancel","status":200,"aborted":false,"ms":11.4,"userId":7,"accountId":42}
{"reqId":"d8da…","method":"PATCH","path":"/orders/5/cancel","status":401,"aborted":false,"ms":1.2}
{"reqId":"1680…","method":"PATCH","path":"/orders/5/cancel","status":400,"aborted":false,"ms":2}
{"reqId":"8567…","method":"PATCH","path":"/orders/5/slow","status":null,"aborted":true,"ms":101.3,"userId":7,"accountId":42}
```

## Why it's built this way

- **Registered first, but writes its line at the end.** By the time the line is written, `sessionUser` has already set `req.user`, so you get the user and account IDs. Registering it before `express.json()` and `sessionUser` also means 401s and malformed-JSON 400s get logged; register it later and those are silently skipped.
- **Uses `'close'` rather than `'finish'`.** `'finish'` never fires if the client disconnects first. With `'close'`, those requests show up with `aborted: true` and a long duration, which is how the silent database hang from last time would appear. `status` is `null` in that case because `res.statusCode` defaults to 200 even when nothing was sent.
- **Logs only fields it names.** The session cookie is enough on its own to log in (`auth.js` looks sessions up by token alone), so headers are never logged wholesale. Bodies are left out because `billing_email` appears in the PATCH body and in the `RETURNING *` response. Query strings are stripped as well.
- **Writes JSON.** `JSON.stringify` escapes newlines, so a crafted URL or header can't forge extra log lines.
- **Carries a request ID.** It reuses an upstream `X-Request-Id` only if the value looks well-formed, and sends it back in the response. That lets you match a user's bug report to a specific log line.

## What it won't catch

- **Requests that crash the process.** As covered last time, a dropped connection inside `write()` kills the process before `'close'` fires; so does any failed async handler on Express 4. Those are exactly the requests you'd most want logged, so that fix comes first.
- **Why a request failed.** The line says 500 but not why. You'd still need an Express error handler that logs the error along with `req.id`. Express's default handler just prints the stack to stderr, which is the `SyntaxError` line the test printed.
- **Database errors between requests.** `pool.on('error')` fires with no request attached, so those log lines won't have a request ID.

## Optional additions

- **Request IDs on database errors.** If you want errors from `db.write()` tagged with the request ID without passing `req` around, use `AsyncLocalStorage`. The middleware stores `req.id` in it, and `db.js` reads it back when it logs.
- **pino-http instead.** If you'd rather have a full logger (with levels and transports), pino-http does the same job. Its default output includes request headers, though, so configure it to drop `req.headers.cookie` or you'll log session tokens.

Request logs also aren't an audit trail. If you need a record of who deleted an account, write it to a table inside the same transaction in `write()`.

