Since `accounts.js` and `orders.js` are both routers mounted on the same `app` in `app.js`, the right place for this is one centralized middleware there — not something added inside each route file. Sketch:

```js
// app.js
const { randomUUID } = require("crypto");

function requestLogger(req, res, next) {
  req.id = req.headers["x-request-id"] || randomUUID();
  const start = process.hrtime.bigint();

  console.log(JSON.stringify({ event: "request.start", id: req.id, method: req.method, path: req.originalUrl }));

  res.on("finish", () => {
    const ms = Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - start) / 1e6;
    console.log(JSON.stringify({
      event: "request.finish",
      id: req.id,
      method: req.method,
      path: req.originalUrl,
      status: res.statusCode,
      durationMs: ms,
      userId: req.user && req.user.id,
      accountId: req.user && req.user.accountId,
    }));
  });

  next();
}

app.use(express.json());
app.use(requestLogger);
app.use(sessionUser);
app.use("/accounts", accounts);
app.use("/orders", orders);
```

A few placement/design choices worth calling out:

- **Mount it before `sessionUser`, but log on `res.on("finish")`, not synchronously on the way in.** That way even requests that get rejected by `sessionUser` (401s) or 404s get logged, and by the time `finish` fires, `req.user` has already been populated (if auth succeeded) — so you get `userId`/`accountId` on the same line without having to thread anything through the route handlers.
- **Log both start and finish, not just finish.** This connects to the dead-connection issue from before: if a request hangs forever because `write()` throws mid-transaction and nothing ever calls `res.json`/`res.status`, you'd see a `request.start` line with no matching `request.finish` — that asymmetry is exactly the signal an operator needs to notice "this request never completed" rather than just silence.
- **Include a request id** (`req.id`, honoring an incoming `x-request-id` if present) so a single request's start/finish/error lines can be correlated in aggregated logs, and so it can be threaded into any DB error logging in `db.js` later.
- **Don't log `req.body` wholesale.** `accounts.js` bodies carry `billing_email`/`display_name`, and cookies carry the session token — log structured metadata (method, path, status, duration, user/account id) rather than raw bodies or headers, to avoid leaking PII/secrets into logs.
- **One logger, not per-router.** Because both routers live under the same `app`, a single `app.use(requestLogger)` before the routers covers `/accounts/*` and `/orders/*` (and anything mounted later) automatically — no need to duplicate logging calls inside each route handler.
- For anything beyond ad hoc `console.log`, a structured logger like `pino` (with `pino-http` for the request-id/timing wiring) is the standard swap-in here — same shape, but with log levels, JSON output, and better performance than hand-rolled `console.log`.
- This pairs naturally with adding an error-handling middleware (4-arg `(err, req, res, next)`) at the end of the chain, so thrown errors — like the `ROLLBACK`-masked error from `write()` discussed earlier — get logged with the same request id instead of surfacing as a silent hang or an unhandled rejection. That's a separate change from logging itself, just flagging the connection.

